    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "The length of the diagonal of a rectangular prism can be found using the Pythagorean Theorem. The formula is:\n\n\\[d = \\sqrt{l^2 + w^2 + h^2}\\]\n\nwhere \\(d\\) is the length of the diagonal, \\(l\\) is the length of the longest side (the dimension that is occurring 3 times), \\(w\\) is the width, and \\(h\\) is the height.\n\nIn this situation, one diagonal of the rectangular prism connects two opposite corners in the top face. We will treat the 10-inch sides of the prism as the operating sides, and the length of the longest diagonal would occur on the last dimension.\n\nHence,\n\n\\[d = \\sqrt{10^2 + 10^2 + 20^2} = \\sqrt{100 + 100 + 400} = \\sqrt{600}\\]\n\n\\[\\boxed{10\\sqrt{6}}\\]",
