    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find the length of the diagonal connecting point A and point B, we can use the Pythagorean theorem. First, let's identify the lengths of the sides connected by the diagonal.\n\nThe diagonal of a rectangular prism connecting point A and point B can be seen as the hypotenuse of a right triangle. The legs of this triangle are two adjacent rectangular faces, with measurements from the surface of the rectangular prism. Specifically, the lengths of the two adjacent rectangular faces are:\n\n1. Opposite face: length = \\( 20 \\), width = \\( 10 \\)\n2. Adjacent face: length = \\( 10 \\), width = \\( 10 \\)\n\nThe diagonal \\( d \\) of the rectangular prism can be computed by applying the Pythagorean theorem twice. First, compute the diagonal connecting points A and B on the left/right and top/bottom side of the prism:\n\n\\[ \\text{Left/Right Diagonal} = \\sqrt{(20-10)^2 + 10^2} = \\sqrt{10^2 + 10^2} = \\sqrt{200} = 10\\sqrt{2} \\]\n\nNext, compute the diagonal connecting point A and point B on the front/back side of the prism:\n\n\\[ \\text{Front/Back Diagonal} = \\sqrt{20^2 + 10^2} = \\sqrt{400 + 100} = \\sqrt{500} = 10\\sqrt{5} \\]\n\nFinally, the total diagonal \\( d \\) connecting all dimensions is:\n\n\\[ d = \\sqrt{(10\\sqrt{2})^2 + (10\\sqrt{5})^2} = \\sqrt{200 + 500} = \\sqrt{700} = 10\\sqrt{7} \\]\n\nShowing this in Python code: \n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define a function to compute the length of the diagonal\ndef diagonal_rectangular_prism(opposite_face, adjacent_face):\n    return sp.sqrt((opposite_face - adjacent_face)**2 + adjacent_face**2)\n\n# Define the dimensions of the rectangular prism\nopposite_face = 20\nadjacent_face = 10\n\n# Compute the diagonal\ndiagonal = diagonal_rectangular_prism(opposite_face, adjacent_face)\n\n# Simplify and display the results\nprint(diagonal)\n```",
